  FYI: Using 'diff' will give results that can be (more or less) 
automatically added using 'patch'.  The actual 'diff' command 
line should be included in the 'patch' file, as 'patch' is rather
good about understand 'diff' output.  An example line for diff'ing
an individual file would be:


diff -NurbBX ignore.txt Pristine/src/mapglyph.c Prompt_Fix/src/mapglyph.c


  "Pristine" and "Prompt_Fix_New" are subdirectories containing 
the directory trees of a pristine Nethack source tree and (my) 
prompt_fix Nethack source tree.  (The pristine Nethack source tree
is used by 'diff' for reference to base its changes on.)

  The 'X' and "ignore.txt" are not necessary when comparing 
individual files, but are included for completeness.  

  "ignore.txt" contains files to be ignored by diff (as signaled
by the 'X' flag).  This permits source trees containing the binary 
files to be compared.

  '-NurbB' is a magic incantation whose purpose I forgot as soon 
as I figured out that it should be used.  (I have a 'makepatch' 
file to remember the flags & syntax for me.)


=========== Contents of makepatch ===============
diff -NurbBX ignore.txt "%1" "%2"
=========== End contents of makepatch ===============


=========== Contents of ignore.txt ===============
*.exe
*.o
*.dat
*.map
*.tds
logfile
nhdat
record
*.dll
date.h
*.bak
*.tag
=========== End contents of ignore.txt ===============


  Feel free to modify ignore.txt and makepatch as appropriate
for your development environment.


  'diff' and 'patch' should be available on any unix system.
(If not, you're sol as far as I'm concerned.)

  'diff' for Misfot Windows can be obtained (along with the
GNU C compiler for Windows) from <http://www.djgpp.com>.



